============================================================================
THE ENTROPY FRONT AT HIGH RELATIVE VELOCITY
============================================================================

  c_s   = sqrt(3 alpha) c = 0.1479596 c = 44,357 km/s
  step  = 6.41 keV, of which a traversing element takes the kinetic
          third = 2137 eV at full traversal

[1] The recorded slow-limit numbers, reproduced
    ordinary infall, 1000 km/s     f = 0.0225 (booked 0.023)   dE =  48.2 eV (booked 50.0)
    merger shocks, ~3000 km/s      f = 0.0676 (booked 0.07)   dE = 144.5 eV (booked 150.0)

    The slow-limit law is reproduced, so the extension below is an extension
    of the corpus's own construction and not a substitute for it.

[2] Where the linear law dies
    f = v/c_s is a TRAVERSED FRACTION. A fraction cannot exceed one, so the
    law is only meaningful while v < c_s, and the correct statement is

        f = min(1, v/c_s),      dE = (E_step/3) * f

    Saturation at v = c_s = 44,357 km/s gives the ceiling
        dE_max = E_step/3 = 2137 eV per particle
    which in the file's own units is ~2137 keV cm^2 --
    above the 100-300 keV cm^2 floors that groups actually show.

    So the ceiling is NOT small. The reason the channel stays sub-dominant is
    entirely that nothing gets near the ceiling, which is the next section.

[3] Why the saturation velocity and the dissipation threshold are the same
    Two independent statements both land on c_s:
      (a) f = v/c_s reaches 1 at v = c_s   -- the element traverses the whole front
      (b) Landau's criterion on the Bogoliubov branch gives v_c = c_s exactly
          (no roton minimum; see medium_induced_decoherence.py)

    They are the same number because both are the speed at which the medium
    re-phases. Below it the medium keeps ahead of the element, the pickup is
    adiabatic, and Landau forbids any excitation -- the entropy gained is
    reversible work, not dissipation. At and above it the medium can no longer
    keep ahead, and the same crossing that saturates the fraction also opens
    the excitation channel. The slow-limit treatment is therefore not merely
    an approximation that degrades; it describes a qualitatively different
    regime from the one above threshold, and there is a sharp line between.

[4] What actually reaches it
    Threshold: 44,357 km/s = 0.1480 c

      system                          v (km/s)     v/c_s     regime
      cool-core infall                      800     0.018   subcritical
      cluster infall, typical             1,000     0.023   subcritical
      merger shock, typical               3,000     0.068   subcritical
      Bullet Cluster shock                4,700     0.106   subcritical
      fastest known cluster merger        5,000     0.113   subcritical
      AGN outflow, slow end (0.03c)       8,994     0.203   subcritical
      AGN ultra-fast outflow (0.1c)      29,979     0.676   subcritical
      AGN ultra-fast outflow (0.3c)      89,938     2.028   SUPERCRITICAL
      relativistic jet (Gamma ~ 10)     298,293     6.725   SUPERCRITICAL

    Every thermal-gas system in the universe is subcritical, and by a wide
    margin: the fastest cluster merger known reaches v/c_s = 0.113.
    Its pickup is 241 eV, i.e. ~241 keV cm^2 -- inside the
    observed 100-300 floor range, but still a few per cent of what the shock
    itself supplies, exactly as the file already argues.

[5] The one place the threshold is crossed by bulk matter
    The threshold, 0.148 c, sits INSIDE the observed range of AGN
    ultra-fast outflow velocities, which run from about 0.03 c to 0.3 c.
    So the model divides a single observed population in two: outflows below
    0.148 c move through the medium without exciting it, and outflows above
    that velocity can. Relativistic jets are far above and always supercritical.

    This is a sharp, model-specific velocity applied to a population that is
    already measured and already binned by velocity. Whether the crossing is
    OBSERVABLE is a separate question and needs the matter-medium coupling,
    which is not yet fixed -- so no detectability claim is made here.
    What is fixed is where the line falls.

============================================================================
VERDICT on #65
============================================================================

    The owed treatment is supplied, and the answer is that the regime the file was
    worried about is never reached by the gas the file is about.

      * The linear law f = v/c_s is a traversed fraction and saturates at f = 1.
        Its ceiling is E_step/3 = 2137 eV per particle, which is ABOVE the observed
        100-300 keV cm^2 floors. The channel is bounded by the ceiling, not by the
        law being small.

      * Saturation and the Landau threshold are the same velocity, c_s, because both
        measure how fast the medium re-phases. Below it the pickup is adiabatic and
        Landau forbids excitation; above it the pickup saturates and dissipation opens.

      * Every thermal-gas system is subcritical. The fastest cluster merger known
        reaches v/c_s = 0.113, so the slow-limit treatment is valid everywhere the
        corpus applies it. The high-velocity correction to cluster entropy is nil.

      * The threshold IS crossed by AGN ultra-fast outflows, whose measured velocities
        straddle 0.148 c, and by relativistic jets. That is where any high-velocity
        physics lives, and it is a different observational programme from the entropy
        floor entirely.

    Net: #65 closes for clusters by showing the correction vanishes there, and hands
    the surviving question to a population the entropy file never considered.

